/-!
# Descent along a covering family for the presheaf cokernel of a presentation

Let a sheaf of modules on a slice of the fppf site associated with a category fibred in groupoids
over a big fppf category of schemes be presented by a morphism `a` between two coproducts of
copies of the structure sheaf, and let `π` exhibit a presheaf of modules as the cokernel of `a`
computed object by object.  This file proves that restriction to the members of a covering family
of an affine object is *injective* on the sections of that presheaf cokernel, and reduces the
sheaf condition for such a family to the case of a family which is fppf in schemes.

The proof uses neither a coproduct of the family inside the site nor any cohomology.  Over a
single covering morphism `Y ⟶ Z` of affine objects the sections of the presheaf cokernel over `Y`
are the base change of its sections over `Z` along the induced map of rings of global functions
(`SheafOfModules.exists_baseChangeEquiv_of_isPresheafCokernel`, whose bases come from
`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.tautBasisOfIsAffineOverCoeffs`), and the base change carries
`1 ⊗ₜ n` to the restriction of `n`.  Applying this to each member of a finite family separately
turns the vanishing of all the restrictions of a section `n` into the vanishing of `1 ⊗ₜ n` in
the base change along every member; the tensor product commutes with a finite product of rings,
so `1 ⊗ₜ n` vanishes in the base change along the product of the rings of global functions of the
members; and that product is faithfully flat over the ring of global functions on the object
covered, because the family is flat and jointly surjective
(`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.faithfullyFlat_pi_gamma_of_ofArrows_mem_fppfPrecoverage`).  So `n`
vanishes.

An arbitrary covering family need not be flat — a covering sieve of an affine object contains
finite families of affine objects with non-flat members — so the conclusion for an arbitrary
covering family is obtained by refining it.  A covering sieve contains a finite family of affine
objects whose image in schemes is an fppf covering family
(`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.exists_finite_affine_fppf_ofArrows_le_over`), and each member of
the refinement factors through a member of the given family, so a section whose restrictions
along the given family vanish has vanishing restrictions along the refinement.  The order matters:
a family obtained by refining an fppf family by affine objects is again fppf, while refining an
affine family cannot make it flat.

The second half of the file is the other half of the sheaf condition for a covering family, and it
is reduced to the same fppf case by the same refinement.  A sheaf condition does not descend along
a refinement by itself — an amalgamation produced over the refinement restricts correctly along
the refined arrows, not along the given ones — but it does once the presheaf is separated for the
covering sieves of the *members* of the given family, which are affine, and that separatedness is
what the injectivity above supplies through
`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSeparatedFor_affine_of_injective_map`.  The amalgamation over the
refinement and the given section over a member agree after restriction along every arrow of the
pullback along that member of the sieve generated by the refinement, which is a covering sieve of
an affine object; so they agree.

What remains for the fppf case is the sheaf condition for one finite family which is fppf in
schemes, and it is stated here in the form degree-zero exactness of an Amitsur complex supplies:
a faithfully flat algebra whose base change of the sections over the object covered is the product
of the sections over the members, an injection of the next term of the Amitsur complex into the
product of the sections over the pairwise fibre products, and the identification of the
degree-zero Amitsur differential with the difference of the two restrictions to those fibre
products.  Nothing in that statement is about schemes, and nothing in it is a sheaf.

## Main results

* `Module.FaithfullyFlat.eq_zero_of_forall_one_tmul_eq_zero`: **an element of a module whose image
  in the base change along every factor of a finite product of algebras vanishes is zero**, when
  the product is faithfully flat.
* `PresheafOfModules.injective_map_of_forall_injective_baseChange`: **restriction to the members
  of a finite family is injective on the sections of a presheaf of modules over an object**, as
  soon as those sections inject, member by member, into the sections over the member through a
  base change which takes `1 ⊗ₜ n` to the restriction of `n`, and the product of the coefficient
  rings is faithfully flat.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.injective_map_of_isPresheafCokernel_of_fppf`: **restriction to the
  members of a finite family of affine objects whose image in schemes is an fppf covering family
  is injective on the sections of the presheaf cokernel of a presentation over an affine object.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.injective_map_of_isPresheafCokernel`: the same for an arbitrary
  covering family of an affine object, flatness no longer being assumed.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.injective_map_cokernel_of_isAffineOver`: **the same for the
  categorical cokernel of the presenting morphism computed in presheaves of modules**, which is
  the injectivity hypothesis of
  `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.surjective_val_app_of_isSheafFor_affine_of_isColimit`.
* `PresheafOfModules.isSheafFor_ofArrows_of_amitsurDiff₀`: **the sheaf condition for a covering
  family**, from degree-zero exactness of the Amitsur complex of a faithfully flat algebra
  together with an identification of the sections over the members of the family with the degree
  one term of that complex and an injection of its degree two term into the sections over the
  pairwise fibre products.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheafFor_affine_of_isSheafFor_fppf`: **the sheaf condition for a
  covering family of affine objects of an affine object follows from the sheaf condition for the
  finite covering families of affine objects whose image in schemes is an fppf covering family**,
  given separatedness at the affine objects.
* `Module.BaseChange.exists_piAddEquiv`: **the base change of a module along a finite product of
  algebras is the product of its base changes along the factors.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.algebra_isPushout_appTop_of_isPullback` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.algebra_isPushout_gamma_of_isPullback`: **the ring of global
  functions on a fibre product of affine schemes, and of affine objects of a slice of the
  associated fppf site, is the tensor product of the rings of global functions of the two factors
  over that of the base.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.isSheafFor_cokernel_affine_of_isSheafFor_fppf`: the same for the
  presheaf cokernel of a presentation, where the separatedness is discharged by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.injective_map_cokernel_of_isAffineOver`, so that **the sheaf
  condition asked for by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.surjective_val_app_of_isSheafFor_affine_of_isColimit` reduces to
  the case of a family which is fppf in schemes.**

## Implementation notes

The commutative rings are the rings of global functions on the underlying schemes, and the
module structures on the sections over them are the structures over the rings of sections of the
coefficient sheaf of rings, which are the same rings definitionally
(`AlgebraicGeometry.FibredSite.ringStructureSheafCore_over_obj_obj`).  Over a member of the family
the ring of global functions on the object covered acts through the induced map of global
functions, so that the restriction maps are linear rather than only semilinear; the algebra
structure is the one the induced map of global functions defines, so the equation identifying its
`algebraMap` with that map, which both the faithful flatness and the scalar towers are stated
against, holds by `rfl`.

The statements are for a morphism `π` with the property
`PresheafOfModules.IsPresheafCokernel` rather than for the categorical cokernel, which is a
`noncomputable` construction and so cannot appear in the definitions of the module structures the
proof introduces; the categorical cokernel has the property by
`PresheafOfModules.isPresheafCokernel_cokernelπ`, which is how the last statement is obtained
from the one before it.

Both coproducts of the presentation carry their tautological generators and the witnesses that
they are coproducts, because the base-change comparison for the presheaf cokernel uses the
generators of the relations as well as the generators of the module.

